The equation of motion is given by

$$
\begin{aligned}
F & =\frac{d}{d t}(\gamma m v) \\
& =\frac{m c \dot{\beta}}{\left(1-\beta^{2}\right)^{\frac{3}{2}}}
\end{aligned}
\tag{1}
$$

$$
F=\gamma^{3} m a
\tag{2}
$$

where $\gamma=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-\beta^{2}}}$ and $\beta=\frac{v}{c}$. So the acceleration is given by

$$
a=\frac{F}{\gamma^{3} m}
\tag{3}
$$

Eq.(3) can be rewritten as

$$
\begin{aligned}
c \frac{d \beta}{d t} & =\frac{F}{\gamma^{3} m} \\
\int_{0}^{\beta} \frac{d \beta}{\left(1-\beta^{2}\right)^{\frac{3}{2}}} & =\frac{F}{m c} \int_{0}^{t} d t \\
\frac{\beta}{\sqrt{1-\beta^{2}}} & =\frac{F t}{m c}
\end{aligned}
\tag{4}
$$

$$
\beta=\frac{\frac{F t}{m c}}{\sqrt{1+\left(\frac{F t}{m c}\right)^{2}}}
\tag{5}
$$

Using Eq.(5), we get

$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_{0}^{x} d x & =\int_{0}^{t} \frac{F t d t}{m \sqrt{1+\left(\frac{F t}{m c}\right)^{2}}} \\
x & =\frac{m c^{2}}{F}\left(\sqrt{1+\left(\frac{F t}{m c}\right)^{2}}-1\right) .
\end{aligned}
\tag{6}
$$

Eq.(3) can also be rewritten as

$$
c \frac{d \beta}{\gamma d \tau}=\frac{g}{\gamma^{3}}
\tag{14}
$$

$$
\int_{0}^{\beta} \frac{d \beta}{1-\beta^{2}} =\frac{g}{c} \int_{0}^{\tau} d \tau
$$
$$
\ln \left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-\beta^{2}}}+\frac{\beta}{\sqrt{1-\beta^{2}}}\right) =\frac{g \tau}{c}
\tag{15}
$$
$$
\sqrt{\frac{1+\beta}{1-\beta}} =e^{\frac{g \tau}{c}}
$$

$$
\beta\left(e^{\frac{g \tau}{c}}+e^{-\frac{g \tau}{c}}\right) =e^{\frac{g \tau}{c}}-e^{-\frac{g \tau}{c}}
$$

$$
\beta =\tanh \frac{g \tau}{c} .
\tag{16}
$$

The time dilation relation is

$$
d t=\gamma d \tau
\tag{17}
$$

From eq.(16), we have

$$
\gamma=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-\beta^{2}}}=\cosh \frac{g \tau}{c}
\tag{18}
$$

Combining this equations, we get

$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_{0}^{t} d t & =\int_{0}^{\tau} d \tau \cosh \frac{g \tau}{c} \\
t & =\frac{c}{g} \sinh \frac{g \tau}{c} .
\end{aligned}
\tag{19}
$$

For a magnetic moment making an angle of $\phi$ with $\mathbf{B}$,

$$
